/**
 * AF3's reference conformers for the 21 protein components, baked.
 *
 * Generated by tools/oracle/dump_reference_conformers.py - do not hand-edit.
 *
 * The browser cannot run AF3's featuriser: it needs a 515 MB chemical component
 * dictionary. These are the 21 entries a protein chain actually reaches, taken
 * from that featuriser rather than rebuilt from a chemistry library, so the atom
 * order, the dense-slot indices, the four-character names, the element codes and
 * the formal charges are AF3's by construction.
 *
 * 🔴 AF3 SAMPLES A FRESH CONFORMER PER RESIDUE INSTANCE and this is ONE sample.
 * Bond lengths and angles are fixed; torsions are not. Folding 6MRR with one
 * conformer per residue type instead of AF3's own moves the trunk's pair by
 * relRMS 2.7e-2 and the structure by 0.01 A - the model was trained to ignore
 * the torsions, which is why a constant is admissible.
 *
 * 🔴 THE C-TERMINAL RESIDUE CARRIES AN OXT AND NO OTHER RESIDUE DOES. It is the
 * only difference between the three positional forms - the N-terminal form is
 * the internal one - and it is one atom in 574 that lands in the last token's
 * pooled atom representation and in the atom-pair window around it.
 *
 * Each atom is [slot, name, element, charge, x, y, z]: `slot` is its index in
 * the 24-wide dense layout, which is not always its position in this list.
 *
 * `rigid` is [i, j, distance] over indices into `internal`, listing the atom
 * pairs whose separation AF3 holds FIXED across its resampled conformers -
 * bonds and 1-3 angles, read off the bond graph rather than guessed from a
 * distance cutoff, and verified against ten AF3 conformers per type. It is what lets a checker hold this table to AF3's
 * chemistry while letting the torsions go, and it is why an aromatic ring has
 * far more rigid pairs than a lysine chain of the same size.
 */

